<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Topiltzin Quetzalcoatl</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ce Acatl Topiltzin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Topiltzin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl">Quetzalcoatl</a>, also called Ce Acatl from his birth date, was a priest-king of Tula remembered in later Aztec and other Mesoamerican traditions as the ideal ruler of the Toltecs. The stories say he opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>, was tricked into disgrace by the god Tezcatlipoca and left Tula for the east, promising to return. Historians treat the accounts as legend that may preserve memories of real rulers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/topiltzin-quetzalcoatl.jpg" alt="Topiltzin Quetzalcoatl" width="319" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: author unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quetzalcoatl_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The full name, Cē Ācatl Topiltzin Quetzalcōātl, means Our Prince One-Reed Precious Serpent. He appears in 16th-century accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> historical traditions, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, who saw the Toltecs as their predecessors in the region, placed him in the 10th century, though the dates given for his life vary between accounts. One version has him born in what is now Tepoztlán. Sources name four possible fathers, most often Mixcōatl, the god of war, fire and the hunt and presumably also an earlier Toltec king; his mother is usually given as Chimalman.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Few accounts tell of his childhood, but all agree that he proved himself first as a warrior and then as a priest of Tōllan. As lord of the Toltecs he is said to have reigned in peace and prosperity and to have been held a god on earth by his followers. The tradition credits him with ending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>, so that the Toltecs offered snakes, birds and other animals instead, and with founding a cult of the serpent in which worshippers drew their own blood, priests remained celibate and intoxication was forbidden. Later rulers claimed descent from him to legitimise their rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In one version he went in a year 1 Acatl, aged 53, to Tlapallan on the Gulf coast, where he took a canoe and burned himself. Other tales have him travel across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> founding communities and naming places, then cross the sea to the east, vowing to return and reclaim Cholula, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chimalpahin">Chimalpahin</a>, Motolinia and Ixtlilxochitl tell it; Las Casas, Mendieta and Veytia instead wrote that he would send representatives. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>, legend makes him the founder of Cuzcatlán, and Maarten Jansen identifies him with the ruler Four Jaguar of the Mixtec codices.</p><p>He was often conflated with the deity <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl">Quetzalcoatl</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florentine-codex">Florentine Codex</a>, compiled under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardino-de-sahagun">Bernardino de Sahagún</a>, records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a> mistook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> for the returning Quetzalcoatl, but most modern scholars regard this as one of the myths of the conquest that arose in the early post-conquest period. He is usually shown with a plumed headpiece, a curved baton and a feather-rimmed shield bearing the wind jewel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cē Ācatl Topiltzin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Colonial chronicles say that the Toltecs combined Tolteca-Chichimeca migrants from the north with the Nonoalca, a people associated with the Gulf Coast, and they credit the city’s rise to leaders such as Mixcoatl and his son <b>Ce Acatl Topiltzin</b> Quetzalcoatl, figures of legend rather than of history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/toltec-state">Toltec state · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tepoztlán, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.99,-99.1" aria-label="Tepoztlán on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Topiltzin Quetzalcoatl"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4267 5907h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4267 5907h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.99,-99.1">What was here? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
